INSERT INTO article (id, slug, path, section, title, h1, meta_description, body_md, word_count, reading_minutes, author, featured_image_id, published_at, modified_at, schema_disabled, is_published) VALUES (97, 'oven-symbol-for-grill', 'tips-tricks/oven-symbol-for-grill', 'tips-tricks', 'Oven Symbol for Grill: What Each Icon Means', 'Oven Symbol for Grill: What Each Icon Means', 'Confused by the oven symbol for grill on your oven? Learn what each oven grill icon means and when to use it for perfect results every time.', 'Have you ever stood in front of your oven, finger hovering over the dial, and thought to yourself: what on earth does that symbol actually mean? You are not alone. Oven control panels are full of mysterious icons, and the grill symbols can be some of the most confusing ones to decode. Whether you just got a new [built-in oven](/built-in-oven/) or you have been cooking with yours for years, knowing exactly which oven symbol for grill to use makes a real difference in your cooking results. In this guide, we break down each grill symbol, what it does, and when to use it.

![Oven Symbol for Grill](https://pub-d0b729df0b8f422289c6f46d17d33f3e.r2.dev/2026/04/Oven-Symbol-for-Grill.webp)

## Why Oven Symbols Can Be So Confusing

One of the most common frustrations with modern ovens is that symbols are not fully standardised across brands. A grill icon on a Vatti built-in oven may look slightly different from the same function on another brand, even though they do the same thing. Add in the fact that newer multifunction ovens come with six, eight, or even more cooking modes, and it is easy to feel overwhelmed.

The good news is that once you understand what each grill symbol is trying to tell you, the logic behind them starts to make sense. Most oven symbols use simple visual cues: zigzag lines typically represent the grill element at the top of the oven, a fan icon represents air circulation, and combinations of these give you a clue about how the two work together. If you are ever unsure, your oven manual is always your best reference for brand-specific icons.

With that said, here are the most common grill symbols you will find on a built-in oven, including what they look like and how to use them.

## The Full Grill Symbol

The full grill symbol typically looks like a zigzag or squiggly line across the top of a square or rectangle. On some ovens it appears as a series of three or four peaks, like a row of mountains sitting at the top of the oven cavity illustration.

When you select this function, your oven activates the top heating element at full power. The heat comes directly from above, making this the go-to setting for browning food, melting cheese, gratinating dishes, and giving proteins like chicken thighs or fish fillets a nicely caramelised crust on top.

A few things to keep in mind when using the full grill function:

- Always position your food close to the top heating element for maximum browning, usually on the second or third rack from the top.

- Keep a close eye on your food as the full grill works fast and can go from golden to burnt quickly.

- The full grill is not ideal for thick cuts of meat, as the outside can char before the inside is fully cooked.

## The Part Grill (Half Grill) Symbol

The part grill symbol looks similar to the full grill symbol but with a shorter or smaller zigzag line, sometimes centred or only covering half of the top of the oven illustration. You might also see it described as a half grill or partial grill on some models.

This setting activates only part of the grill element, usually the central section, at a lower heat intensity. It is the smarter choice when you are cooking a smaller portion and do not want to waste energy heating the entire grill area. Think of it as the full grill’s more economical sibling.

The part grill is great for toasting a small tray of vegetables, browning the top of a single-serve pasta bake, or gently melting cheese over a smaller dish. Because it operates at reduced power, there is a bit more margin for error compared to the full grill, which makes it a friendlier option if you are new to grilling in an oven.

## The Fan Grill Symbol

The fan grill symbol combines the zigzag grill line at the top with a fan icon, usually shown as a circular fan shape in the centre or at the bottom of the symbol. On some [combi ovens](/steamer-combi-oven/) , this function is one of the most useful ones you will use.

When you select fan grill, the oven alternates between the grill element and the circulating fan. What this means in practical terms is that hot air is blown around your food at intervals, cooking it more evenly than a standard grill alone. Think of it as a cross between grilling and roasting, similar to how meat cooks on a rotisserie spit.

This makes fan grill the ideal setting for larger cuts of meat, whole chicken, pork fillets, or thick fish. The fan ensures the heat penetrates through the food rather than just searing the very top surface, reducing the risk of a burnt exterior with a raw interior. It also tends to be slightly more forgiving than the full grill, so you have a little more time before needing to check on your food.

## The Grill with Lower Heat Symbol

This symbol is less common, but you will find it on multifunction ovens with more advanced settings. It typically shows a zigzag line at the top combined with a straight line or element symbol at the bottom of the oven illustration, representing both the top grill element and the lower heating element working at the same time.

This combination mode is designed for thicker cuts of meat that need to be cooked through without charring the outside. By adding gentle heat from below, the oven helps cook the food from both ends, which is particularly useful for things like a thick rack of ribs, a whole fish, or a substantial piece of roast.

If your oven has this setting, it is worth experimenting with it for longer grill sessions where you want a proper cook-through with a nicely browned top, without having to flip your food halfway through.

## A Quick Comparison: Which Grill Symbol to Use?

Here is a simple breakdown to help you decide which grill setting to reach for based on what you are cooking:

| Grill Symbol | Best For | Avoid For |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Full Grill | Browning, melting cheese, thin cuts | Thick meats, delicate fish |
| Part Grill | Small portions, gentle browning | Large trays, thick cuts |
| Fan Grill | Whole chicken, roasts, thick fillets | Thin, delicate items |
| Grill with Lower Heat | Thick cuts needing full cook-through | Quick browning tasks |

## Practical Tips for Using the Grill Function on a Built-In Oven

Understanding the symbols is step one. Getting the most out of your grill function is step two. Here are a few simple tips that apply no matter which grill setting you are using:

- Preheat the grill first. Give the grill element a couple of minutes to reach full heat before placing your food inside. This ensures you get immediate browning rather than slow, uneven cooking.

- Use the right rack position. For the full grill and part grill, place food closer to the top element. For fan grill, a middle rack position often works better as the circulating air handles heat distribution.

- Leave the oven door slightly ajar if recommended by your oven manual. Some built-in ovens are designed to be used with the door slightly open during grilling to allow moisture to escape and prevent the thermostat from switching the grill off prematurely.

- Never walk away from the grill. Unlike a fan oven where you can set a timer and come back, grilling requires your attention. The difference between perfectly golden and overdone can be just a couple of minutes.

- Use a grill tray or oven-safe rack. Elevating your food on a rack allows heat to circulate underneath, giving you a better texture on meats and vegetables.
If you are in the market for a built-in oven that makes navigating these functions intuitive, Vatti ovens are worth exploring. The [Vatti Built-In Oven range](/built-in-oven/) features clearly labelled controls and multiple cooking modes, including grill functions, that are designed to take the guesswork out of everyday cooking.

## Conclusion

Once you know what each oven symbol for grill actually means, using your oven becomes a lot less intimidating and a lot more fun. The full grill is your quick-browning powerhouse, the part grill gives you precision for smaller dishes, the fan grill is your best friend for larger roasts and whole proteins, and the grill with lower heat is the setting to reach for when you need a thorough cook without burning the outside.

Every oven is a little different, so always cross-check with your model’s manual if you spot a symbol that does not quite match the descriptions above. And if you are thinking about upgrading to a built-in oven with clear, easy-to-use grill functions, the Vatti team is happy to help you find the right fit for your kitchen.

## Final thoughts

Got questions about which Vatti oven is right for you? [WhatsApp Vatti Malaysia](https://web.whatsapp.com/send?phone=60123366082&text=) today and our team will be happy to help.

