INSERT INTO article (id, slug, path, section, title, h1, meta_description, body_md, word_count, reading_minutes, author, featured_image_id, published_at, modified_at, schema_disabled, is_published) VALUES (103, 'how-to-clean-grease-filter', 'tips-tricks/how-to-clean-grease-filter', 'tips-tricks', 'How to Clean Grease Filter: Best Methods Compared', 'How to Clean Grease Filter: The Best Methods Compared', 'Learn how to clean grease filter using the best methods, from baking soda to degreaser, and tackle tough baked-on grease.', 'There is more than one way to clean a grease filter, and the best method depends on how bad the buildup is and what your filter is made of. A light monthly clean is quick, but baked-on grease needs a stronger approach. This guide compares the main methods so you can pick the right one for your kitchen.

![How to Clean Grease Filter](https://pub-d0b729df0b8f422289c6f46d17d33f3e.r2.dev/2026/06/grease-filter-hero.webp)

### Quick Answer: What Is the Best Way to Clean a Grease Filter?

The most reliable method is soaking the filter in **hot water with baking soda and grease-cutting dish soap** , then scrubbing gently. For lighter grease, a **dishwasher cycle** works well; for stubborn buildup, a **boiling-water soak or a dedicated degreaser** does the job. Always match the method to your filter type.

[Enquire With Us](https://wa.me/60123366082)

## First, Know What You Are Cleaning

A grease filter is the metal panel under your hood that traps oil and smoke before they reach the motor and ducting. Most home kitchens use reusable aluminium mesh or stainless steel baffle filters, both of which can be washed. Charcoal filters in ductless hoods are different, they cannot be cleaned and must be replaced instead.

This guide focuses on choosing the best cleaning method rather than the basic steps. If you would like a full walkthrough of removing and washing your filter the standard way, our [step-by-step kitchen hood filter cleaning guide](/tips-tricks/how-to-clean-kitchen-hood-filter/) covers that in detail. Here, we will compare the methods so you know exactly which one to reach for.

## The Main Cleaning Methods, and When to Use Each

Each method suits a different level of grease and a different filter type. Here is how the five most common approaches compare:

| Method | Best For | Effort | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Baking soda soak | Regular grease, all reusable filters | Low | Reliable all-rounder; gentle and cheap |
| Boiling water soak | Heavy, sticky buildup | Medium | Heat melts grease fast; handle with care |
| Dishwasher cycle | Light grease, dishwasher-safe filters | Very low | Hands-off, but check it is safe first |
| Vinegar rinse | Light grease and odors | Low | Best as a finishing rinse, not a deep clean |
| Commercial degreaser | Stubborn, baked-on grease | Medium | Powerful; rinse very thoroughly after |

For most people, the **baking soda soak** handles routine cleaning perfectly. Step up to boiling water or a degreaser only when grease has hardened and a normal soak is not cutting through it.

## How to Tackle Tough, Baked-On Grease

When grease has been left to harden over months, a single soak often will not shift it. The trick is **heat and patience** . Start with a long soak in very hot water (around 60 to 70 degrees Celsius) with extra baking soda, giving it 30 to 40 minutes rather than the usual 15. Heat softens hardened grease so it lifts away with far less scrubbing.

If patches remain, apply a commercial degreaser directly to the spots and let it **dwell for a few minutes** before working it in with a soft brush. Repeat the soak if needed rather than scrubbing harder, since aggressive scrubbing can damage the filter. For very neglected filters, two gentle rounds will always beat one forceful one.

## Natural vs Chemical Cleaners: Which Should You Use?

Natural cleaners like baking soda and vinegar are cheap, safe around food, and effective for everyday grease. Baking soda is mildly alkaline and breaks down greasy residue, while vinegar helps cut light film and freshen odors. They are the sensible default for regular maintenance.

Chemical degreasers are stronger and faster on heavy buildup, which matters in Malaysian kitchens where frequent frying and wok cooking leave thick grease. The trade-off is that they need careful handling and a very thorough rinse so no residue remains. A simple rule: reach for natural cleaners first, and bring in a degreaser only when the grease genuinely demands it. Avoid mixing vinegar and baking soda at the same time, as they cancel each other out.

## Mistakes That Damage Your Grease Filter

A few common habits quietly ruin filters. Using **steel wool or hard wire brushes** scratches the mesh and finish, which then traps grease even faster. Putting a delicate aluminium filter through a hot dishwasher cycle can also dull or warp it, so always confirm yours is dishwasher-safe first.

The most common mistake of all is **reinstalling a filter while it is still damp** . Trapped moisture leads to odors, rust, and even mould, undoing all your hard work. Always let the filter dry completely before it goes back in. Treating the filter gently keeps it working and saves you the cost of replacements.

## Keeping Grease Buildup Down Between Cleans

A little prevention makes every clean easier. Running your hood on a slightly higher setting while frying captures more grease before it settles, and switching it on a minute before you start cooking helps too. Wiping the filter surface with a damp cloth every couple of weeks stops light grease from hardening into a stubborn layer.

Good habits across your whole hood matter as well. Our guide to [range hood maintenance](/tips-tricks/range-hood-maintenance/) covers keeping the fan and ducting in shape, and if lingering smells are an issue, these tips on [avoiding cooking smell in the house](/tips-tricks/how-to-avoid-cooking-smell-in-the-house-fast/) pair nicely with a clean filter. A well-maintained [kitchen hood](/kitchen-hood-in-malaysia/) simply needs less deep cleaning over time.

## Final thoughts

Match the method to the mess: baking soda for routine grease, heat or a degreaser for stubborn buildup, and gentle handling throughout. Want a hood that is easier to keep clean? [**Message us on WhatsApp**](https://web.whatsapp.com/send?phone=60123366082&text=) for a free, tailored recommendation.

